1. **Fix 2 regression test**: a default-constructed watchdog (no `timersEnabled`) under the test binding arms no timers — elapsing past the threshold produces no breadcrumbs and leaves nothing pending. This is the direct unit-level regression for the CI red (before the fix, this test would fail with a pending-timer violation when run under testWidgets... actually in fakeAsync it would just fire a breadcrumb. Hmm.

Let me think about what test genuinely reproduces the failure mode and passes after the fix:

The CI failure shape: testWidgets boot flow → `kBootWatchdog.beginRestore()` → 10s timer pending at test end → `!timersPending` assertion. A unit reproduction: `testWidgets((tester) async { kBootWatchdog.install(); kBootWatchdog.beginRestore(); /* end test */ })` — before fix: FAILS (pending timer). After fix: PASSES. That's a direct, honest reproduction test! I can add it to the boot_watchdog_test.dart as a testWidgets case (the file already initializes the binding).

Note: `kBootWatchdog` is the process-wide singleton — using it in the test file mutates global state across tests. The singleton's `_installed` flag once set stays set; `beginRestore`/`restoreDone` — as long as I call `restoreDone()` at the end, state is clean. But the singleton is shared with... only this test file's process (each test file runs in its own isolate). Within this file, other tests construct their own watchdogs — no interference. But test ORDER: `testWidgets` cases run interleaved with plain `test`s? In flutter_test, plain `test()` and `testWidgets()` in the same file — the package:test runner runs them in declaration order within the same zone setup... Actually plain tests run in a normal zone, testWidgets in the FakeAsync-ish binding zone; they run sequentially in declaration order. Using the singleton inside one testWidgets case is fine if I clean up (restoreDone + firstFrame). Hmm — but `BootSteps.reset` in setUp/tearDown also touches global. OK.

Safer: construct a FRESH `BootWatchdog()` (default constructor) in the testWidgets case instead of the singleton — the default constructor auto-detects the binding (TestWidgetsFlutterBinding) → timers disabled. That tests exactly the default behavior without singleton pollution:

```dart
testWidgets('under the flutter test binding the default watchdog arms no timers', (tester) async {
  final breadcrumbs = <String>[];
  final watchdog = BootWatchdog(
    threshold: const Duration(seconds: 4),
    restoreThreshold: const Duration(seconds: 10),
    onBreadcrumb: breadcrumbs.add,
  );
  watchdog.install();
  watchdog.firstFrame();
  watchdog.beginRestore();
  // Pre-fix, this test ended with the 10 s restore timer pending — the
  // binding's `!timersPending` invariant killed the boot-flow tests
  // (gh-1507 validation red).
  await tester.pump(const Duration(seconds: 11));
  expect(breadcrumbs, isEmpty);
});
```

Wait — in testWidgets, timers are FakeAsync-driven via `tester.pump(duration)`. Pre-fix: `beginRestore` arms a 10s FakeAsync timer; `pump(11s)` advances the fake clock → the timer FIRES during pump → breadcrumb logged → expect(isEmpty) FAILS (red!), and even without firing, the pending timer would fail the invariant at test end. Either way pre-fix red; post-fix: no timer → no breadcrumb, no pending timer → green. 

